<quiz>
<question type="stack">
  <name><text>Comparing trapezoidal and mid-ordinate estimates</text></name>
  <questiontext format="html">
    <text><![CDATA[<p>The graph shows \(y = {@fx@}\) for \({@a@} \le x \le {@b@}\). A computer gives the exact area under the curve as \({@Ed@}\) (to 2 decimal places). Give exact values.</p><div style="text-align:center;" role="img" aria-label="Graph of y = {#fx#} for {#a#} to {#b#}, with the region between the curve and the x-axis shaded.">[[jsxgraph width="400px" height="280px"]]
(function() {
  var b = JXG.JSXGraph.initBoard(divid, {boundingbox: [{#bxl#}, {#byt#}, {#bxr#}, {#byb#}], axis: true, grid: false, showNavigation: false, showCopyright: false, pan: {enabled: false}, zoom: {enabled: false}});
  var f = function(x) { return {#c0d#} + ({#c1d#})*x + ({#c2d#})*x*x + ({#c3d#})*x*x*x; };
  var lo = {#a#}, hi = {#b#}, X = [lo], Y = [0];
  for (var k = 0; k <= 60; k++) { X.push(lo + (hi - lo) * k / 60); Y.push(f(lo + (hi - lo) * k / 60)); }
  X.push(hi); Y.push(0);
  b.create('curve', [X, Y], {strokeWidth: 0, fillColor: '#bbbbbb', fillOpacity: 0.5, fixed: true, highlight: false});
  b.create('functiongraph', [f, lo, hi], {strokeColor: '#000000', strokeWidth: 2, fixed: true, highlight: false});
  b.create('segment', [[lo, 0], [lo, f(lo)]], {strokeColor: '#000000', strokeWidth: 1, fixed: true, highlight: false});
  b.create('segment', [[hi, 0], [hi, f(hi)]], {strokeColor: '#000000', strokeWidth: 1, fixed: true, highlight: false});
})();
[[/jsxgraph]]</div><p>(a) Find the trapezoidal estimate of the area with \(4\) strips.</p><div>[[input:ans1]] [[validation:ans1]] [[feedback:prt1]]</div><p>(b) Find the mid-ordinate estimate of the area with \(4\) strips.</p><div>[[input:ans2]] [[validation:ans2]] [[feedback:prt2]]</div><p>(c) Which estimate is too big?</p><div>[[input:ans3]] [[validation:ans3]] [[feedback:prt3]]</div><p>(d) Which statement explains this?</p><div>[[input:ans4]] [[validation:ans4]] [[feedback:prt4]]</div>]]></text>
  </questiontext>
  <generalfeedback format="html">
    <text><![CDATA[<p>\(h = ({@b@} - {@a@}) \div 4 = {@exd(hh)@}\).</p><p>(a) Ordinates at the edges: \({@map(exd,ys)@}\). \(T = \tfrac{1}{2} \times {@exd(hh)@} \times \left[{@exd(ys[1])@} + {@exd(ys[5])@} + 2({@exd(ys[2])@} + {@exd(ys[3])@} + {@exd(ys[4])@})\right] = {@exd(Ta)@}\).</p><p>(b) Mid-ordinates at \(x = {@exd(a+hh/2)@}, {@exd(a+3*hh/2)@}, {@exd(a+5*hh/2)@}, {@exd(a+7*hh/2)@}\): \({@map(exd,ms)@}\). \(M = {@exd(hh)@} \times {@exd(apply("+",ms))@} = {@exd(Ma)@}\).</p><p>(c) [[if test="up"]]\(M = {@exd(Ma)@} \lt {@Ed@} \lt T = {@exd(Ta)@}\): the trapezoidal estimate is too big and the mid-ordinate estimate too small.[[else]]\(T = {@exd(Ta)@} \lt {@Ed@} \lt M = {@exd(Ma)@}\): the mid-ordinate estimate is too big and the trapezoidal estimate too small.[[/if]]</p><p>(d) [[if test="up"]]The curve bends upwards, like a bowl: chords lie above a curve that bends upwards, so trapezia overestimate, while each mid-ordinate rectangle leaves out more than it adds.[[else]]The curve bends downwards, like an arch: the chords lie below it, so trapezia underestimate, while each mid-ordinate rectangle adds more than it leaves out.[[/if]] The true area lies between the two estimates, and for these curves the mid-ordinate estimate is the closer one.</p>]]></text>
  </generalfeedback>
  <defaultgrade>4</defaultgrade>
